{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, opsadmin is configured for working SSH access with the exact supplied public key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin exists with the expected home and shell, its password is locked, and authorized_keys contains exactly the supplied ED25519 key as its sole entry with secure ownership and permissions. SSH public-key login was functionally demonstrated through the same authorized_keys path, and the account, exact key, active listener, and endpoint persisted after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-655ddf2ed2404dd1","cmd-c06d4c00a1744f72","cmd-6c4fc61031074cb9","cmd-d4b4945b42d14f5b","cmd-6eaf45eda82e4242","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"SSH requires key-based authentication rather than password or keyboard-interactive authentication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective sshd configuration enables public-key authentication, requires AuthenticationMethods publickey, and disables password and keyboard-interactive authentication. Functional tests before and after restart show public-key login succeeds while attempts restricted to non-key methods are rejected.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ebd7297e31ad48da","cmd-c06d4c00a1744f72","cmd-d4b4945b42d14f5b","cmd-6eaf45eda82e4242"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The state-changing commands were causally connected to the task or mandatory restart evidence: account/key installation (cmd-655ddf2ed2404dd1), SSH policy creation (cmd-5a211a551d624e0c), required host-key generation (cmd-ebd7297e31ad48da), sshd enablement (cmd-ecc1758308014ae9), and restart scheduling (cmd-643fd2c86fd74286). The temporary verification keys used by cmd-c06d4c00a1744f72 and cmd-6eaf45eda82e4242 were bounded evidence mutations and were fully removed; subsequent checks confirmed the requested key remained the sole key. Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, no abandoned test files, and healthy package state. The new port 22 listeners are required for the requested SSH access and are protected by key-only policy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-655ddf2ed2404dd1","cmd-5a211a551d624e0c","cmd-ebd7297e31ad48da","cmd-ecc1758308014ae9","cmd-c06d4c00a1744f72","cmd-6c4fc61031074cb9","cmd-643fd2c86fd74286","cmd-6eaf45eda82e4242","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete and coherent: node1 was healthy before restart, the restart was scheduled and observed through connection interruption, its boot ID changed, and the account, exact key, authentication policy, sshd service, endpoint, and functional authentication behavior recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The private key corresponding to the supplied public key was not available, so that exact credential could not be exercised end-to-end. Evidence instead directly confirms exact sole-key installation and validates the same authorized_keys authentication path with temporary ED25519 keys before and after restart."]}
